 (72/101, √6(-302/303)).\n-302/303 = -302/303. 302=2·151, 303=3·101. \nDirection = (72/101, -302√6/303). Multiply by 303: (72·3, -302√6) = (216, -302√6) = 2·(108, -151√6).\n\nInteresting, direction of XI is (108, -151√6), which is exactly the normal to the radical axis... wait no. The radical axis was 108x-151√6 y+1260=0, normal (108,-151√6). And XI direction is (108,-151√6). So XI is perpendicular to radical axis? No, XI direction = (108,-151√6) which is the normal of radical axis, meaning XI is perpendicular to radical axis AX. \n\nWait, that's interesting. A and X are on the radical axis (line 108x-151√6y+1260=0). The direction of AX is (151√6, 108) (perpendicular to normal (108,-151√6)). And XI has direction (108,-151√6) = normal. So XI ⊥ AX. That means ∠AXI = 90°, i.e., X lies on circle with diameter AI! But that's circle 1, which X is on. Of course! X is on circle with diameter AI, so ∠AXI=90°. Makes sense.\n\nOK so line XI direction (108,-151√6). Parametric from X: X + u·(108,-151√6) = (231/101+108u, 168√6/101-151√6 u).\n\nWait, but I need to be careful with scaling. Let me use direction (108,-151√6) and parameter u, where u=0 gives X.\n\nI corresponds to: 231/101+108u=3 → 108u=3-231/101=72/101 → u=72/(101·108)=72/10908=2/303.\nCheck y: 168√6/101-151√6·2/303 = √6(168/101-302/303)=√6(504/303-302/303)=√6·202/303=√6·202/303. 202/303=2/3. ✓ =2√6/3. Good.\n\nSo I is at u=2/303 on line XI (from X).\n\nNow find P = intersection of line XI with line EF.\n\nLine XI: (231/101+108u, 168√6/101-151√6 u).\nLine EF: (57/35+22s, 36√6/35+√6 s).\n\nSet equal:\n231/101+108u = 57/35+22s ... (1)\n168√6/101-151√6 u = 36√6/35+√6 s ... (2)\n\nFrom (2), divide by √6: 168/101-151u = 36/35+s → s = 168/101-151u-36/35.\n168/101-36/35 = (168·35-36·101)/(101·35) = (5880-3636)/3535 = 2244/3535.\ns = 2244/3535 - 151u.\n\nFrom (1): 108u-22s = 57/35-231/101 = (57·101-231·35)/(35·101) = (5757-8085)/3535 = -2328/3535.\n108u - 22(2244/3535-151u) = -2328/3535.\n108u - 49368/3535 + 3322u = -2328/35